 Let $v = (x,y,z)$ be the coordinates with respect to the standard basis of $\mathbb{R}^3$. Since rotating by an angle $\theta$ about the $z$-axis is essentially a rotation in the $xy$-plane, we simply have
\begin{align*}
\begin{pmatrix}
x' \\
y' \\
z'
\end{pmatrix}
&=
\begin{pmatrix}
\cos\theta & -\sin\theta & 0 \\
\sin\theta & \cos\theta & 0 \\
0 & 0 & 1
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
x \\
y \\
z
\end{pmatrix}
\\
&=
\begin{pmatrix}
\cos\theta & -\sin\theta & 0 \\
\sin\theta & \cos\theta & 0 \\
0 & 0 & 1
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
0 \\
0
\end{pmatrix}
\\
&=
\begin{pmatrix}
\cos\theta \\
\sin\theta \\
0
\end{pmatrix}
\end{align*}
If $\theta = 120^\circ$, we have $x'=\cos(120^\circ) = -\frac{1}{2}$ and $y'=\sin(120^\circ) = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}$. So, the matrix that corresponds to rotating about the origin by an angle of $120^\circ$ counter-clockwise is
\begin{pmatrix}
-\frac{1}{2} & -\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} & 0 \\
\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} & -\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\
0 & 0 & 1
\end{pmatrix}.
